# July – September.
DATA = [
('07-03', 'Saint Thomas the Apostle', 'Saint Thomas the Apostle', '', '1st century, India', ['India', 'architects'],
 'Thomas, called the Twin, doubted the Resurrection until the risen Jesus showed him his wounds. Then he made the great profession of faith: "My Lord and my God!"\n\nTradition holds that he took the Gospel to India, where he was martyred.'),
('07-04', 'Saint Elizabeth of Portugal', 'Saint Elizabeth of Portugal', '1271, Spain', '1336, Portugal', ['peace'],
 'Elizabeth, a princess of Aragon, became Queen of Portugal. She bore an unfaithful husband with patience, served the poor and more than once made peace between warring members of her family.\n\nAfter her husband\'s death she lived as a Franciscan tertiary.'),
('07-05', 'Saint Anthony Zaccaria, priest', 'Saint Anthony Mary Zaccaria', '1502, Cremona, Italy', '1539, Cremona', [],
 'Anthony was a doctor who became a priest and worked to renew the faith in Milan. He founded the Barnabites and spread devotion to the Eucharist and to the letters of Saint Paul. He died at thirty-six.'),
('07-06', 'Saint Maria Goretti, virgin and martyr', 'Saint Maria Goretti', '1890, Italy', '1902, Nettuno, Italy', ['young people', 'purity'],
 'Maria was an eleven-year-old Italian girl who resisted an attack on her purity and was stabbed. Before dying she forgave her attacker, Alessandro.\n\nYears later he repented and was present at her canonisation in 1950.'),
('07-09', 'Saint Augustine Zhao Rong and companions, martyrs', 'Saint Augustine Zhao Rong and Companions', '', '1815 and other years, China', ['China'],
 'Augustine Zhao Rong was a Chinese soldier who, moved by the patience of a martyred bishop, became a Christian and then a priest. He died in prison in 1815.\n\nHe is honoured with 119 other martyrs of China, Chinese and foreign, who died between 1648 and 1930.'),
('07-11', 'Saint Benedict, abbot', 'Saint Benedict', 'c. 480, Nursia, Italy', 'c. 547, Monte Cassino, Italy', ['Europe', 'students', 'against poison'],
 'Benedict left the city of Rome to seek God in solitude. Disciples gathered around him, and at Monte Cassino he wrote his famous Rule, built on prayer and work, *ora et labora*.\n\nBenedictine monasteries preserved faith and learning across Europe. He is patron of Europe.'),
('07-13', 'Saint Henry', 'Saint Henry II', '973, Bavaria', '1024, Germany', ['rulers'],
 'Henry was Holy Roman Emperor who governed with justice, supported the Church, founded monasteries and the Diocese of Bamberg.\n\nWith his wife Saint Cunegunde he was known for his piety.'),
('07-14', 'Saint Camillus de Lellis, priest', 'Saint Camillus de Lellis', '1550, Italy', '1614, Rome', ['the sick', 'nurses', 'hospitals'],
 'A soldier and gambler in his youth, Camillus was converted and devoted himself to caring for the sick. He founded the Ministers of the Sick, whose habit bears a red cross.\n\nHe urged his brothers to care for each patient as if he were Christ himself.'),
('07-15', 'Saint Bonaventure, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Bonaventure', '1221, Italy', '1274, Lyon, France', [],
 'Bonaventure was a Franciscan theologian who taught in Paris alongside Saint Thomas Aquinas. As Minister General he guided the Franciscans wisely, and he wrote the life of Saint Francis.\n\nCalled the "Seraphic Doctor", he died during the Council of Lyon.'),
('07-16', 'Our Lady of Mount Carmel', 'Our Lady of Mount Carmel', '', '', ['Carmelites'],
 'Hermits living on Mount Carmel in the Holy Land formed the Carmelite order under the patronage of the Virgin Mary. Tradition says that in 1251 Mary gave the brown scapular to Saint Simon Stock as a sign of her protection.\n\nMany Catholics wear the scapular as a sign of devotion to Mary.'),
('07-20', 'Saint Apollinaris, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Apollinaris', '', 'Ravenna, Italy', [],
 'Apollinaris was the first Bishop of Ravenna in Italy. He endured persecution and exile for the faith and is honoured as a martyr.'),
('07-21', 'Saint Lawrence of Brindisi, priest and doctor', 'Saint Lawrence of Brindisi', '1559, Brindisi, Italy', '1619, Lisbon, Portugal', [],
 'Lawrence was a Capuchin friar who knew many languages, including Hebrew, and was a powerful preacher across Europe. He served as a peacemaker and even led troops in prayer before battle.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('07-22', 'Saint Mary Magdalene', 'Saint Mary Magdalene', 'Magdala, Galilee', '1st century', ['converts', 'women'],
 'Jesus freed Mary of Magdala from seven demons, and she followed him faithfully, standing at the foot of the cross. On Easter morning she was the first to see the risen Lord, who sent her to tell the apostles.\n\nShe is called the "Apostle to the Apostles". Pope Francis raised her memorial to a feast.'),
('07-23', 'Saint Birgitta, religious', 'Saint Bridget of Sweden', '1303, Sweden', '1373, Rome', ['Europe', 'widows'],
 'Bridget was a Swedish noblewoman, wife and mother of eight. After her husband\'s death she founded the Bridgettine order and moved to Rome, where she urged the popes to reform the Church. Her visions of Christ\'s Passion were widely read.\n\nShe is a patron of Europe.'),
('07-24', 'Saint Sharbel Makhluf, hermit', 'Saint Charbel Makhlouf', '1828, Lebanon', '1898, Lebanon', ['Lebanon'],
 'Charbel was a Maronite monk in Lebanon who spent his last twenty-three years as a hermit, praying and fasting. Many miracles have been reported at his tomb.'),
('07-25', 'Saint James, apostle', 'Saint James the Apostle', 'Galilee', 'c. 44, Jerusalem', ['Spain', 'pilgrims'],
 'James, son of Zebedee and brother of John, was a fisherman called by Jesus. With Peter and John he witnessed the Transfiguration and the agony in Gethsemane.\n\nHe was the first apostle to be martyred, beheaded by King Herod Agrippa. His shrine at Santiago de Compostela in Spain draws pilgrims from around the world.'),
('07-26', 'Saints Joachim and Anne', 'Saints Joachim and Anne', '', '', ['grandparents'],
 'According to ancient tradition, Joachim and Anne were the parents of the Virgin Mary and so the grandparents of Jesus. They are honoured for raising Mary in faith.\n\nPope Francis made the Sunday near this day the World Day for Grandparents and the Elderly.'),
('07-29', 'Saints Martha, Mary and Lazarus', 'Saints Martha, Mary and Lazarus', 'Bethany', '1st century', ['hospitality', 'cooks'],
 'Martha, Mary and their brother Lazarus of Bethany were close friends of Jesus, who welcomed him into their home. Martha served, Mary sat at his feet to listen, and Jesus raised Lazarus from the dead.\n\nMartha professed: "You are the Christ, the Son of God."'),
('07-30', 'Saint Peter Chrysologus, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Peter Chrysologus', 'c. 380, Imola, Italy', 'c. 450, Imola', [],
 'Peter was Bishop of Ravenna whose short, powerful sermons earned him the name Chrysologus, "golden word".\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('07-31', 'Saint Ignatius of Loyola, priest', 'Saint Ignatius of Loyola', '1491, Loyola, Spain', '1556, Rome', ['the Jesuits', 'soldiers', 'retreats'],
 'Ignatius was a Spanish soldier wounded in battle. While recovering, he read lives of Christ and the saints and was converted. He wrote the *Spiritual Exercises* and founded the Society of Jesus, the Jesuits, to serve the Church wherever needed.\n\nHis motto: "For the greater glory of God."'),
('08-01', 'Saint Alphonsus Maria de Liguori, bishop and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Alphonsus Liguori', '1696, Naples, Italy', '1787, Italy', ['confessors', 'moral theologians'],
 'Alphonsus left a successful legal career to become a priest. He founded the Redemptorists to preach to the poor, wrote widely on prayer, the Blessed Virgin and moral theology, and became a bishop.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('08-02', 'Saint Eusebius of Vercelli, bishop', 'Saint Eusebius of Vercelli', '', '371, Vercelli, Italy', [],
 'Eusebius was Bishop of Vercelli in Italy who defended the faith in the divinity of Christ and suffered exile for it. He lived with his clergy in a community of prayer.'),
('08-02', 'Saint Peter Julian Eymard, priest', 'Saint Peter Julian Eymard', '1811, France', '1868, France', ['the Eucharist'],
 'Peter Julian was a French priest, the "Apostle of the Eucharist", who founded the Congregation of the Blessed Sacrament to promote adoration of Christ in the Eucharist.'),
('08-04', 'Saint Jean Vianney (the Curé of Ars), priest', 'Saint John Vianney', '1786, France', '1859, Ars, France', ['parish priests'],
 'John Vianney struggled with his studies but became parish priest of the small village of Ars. Through prayer, penance and long hours in the confessional, up to sixteen hours a day, he transformed the village, and pilgrims came from all over France.\n\nHe is patron of parish priests.'),
('08-07', 'Saint Cajetan, priest', 'Saint Cajetan', '1480, Vicenza, Italy', '1547, Naples, Italy', ['job seekers'],
 'Cajetan founded the Theatines, priests dedicated to renewing the clergy and serving the poor. He trusted completely in God\'s providence.'),
('08-07', 'Saint Sixtus II, pope, and companions, martyrs', 'Saint Sixtus II and Companions', '', '258, Rome', [],
 'Pope Sixtus II was arrested while celebrating the liturgy in the catacombs during the persecution of Valerian and beheaded with four deacons. He is named in the Roman Canon.'),
('08-08', 'Saint Dominic, priest', 'Saint Dominic', '1170, Spain', '1221, Bologna, Italy', ['astronomers'],
 'Dominic founded the Order of Preachers, the Dominicans, to preach the truth of the Gospel with learning and holiness of life. Tradition links him with spreading the Rosary.\n\nHis friars and sisters still preach and teach worldwide.'),
('08-09', 'Saint Teresa Benedicta of the Cross (Edith Stein), virgin and martyr', 'Saint Teresa Benedicta of the Cross (Edith Stein)', '1891, Breslau', '1942, Auschwitz', ['Europe'],
 'Edith Stein was a Jewish philosopher who found the truth in Christ after reading the life of Saint Teresa of Avila. She became a Carmelite nun.\n\nArrested by the Nazis, she was killed at Auschwitz in 1942. She is a patron of Europe.'),
('08-10', 'Saint Lawrence, deacon and martyr', 'Saint Lawrence', 'Spain', '258, Rome', ['deacons', 'cooks', 'the poor'],
 'Lawrence was a deacon of Rome in charge of caring for the poor. When ordered to hand over the Church\'s treasures, he gathered the poor and sick and said: "These are the treasures of the Church."\n\nTradition says he was martyred on a gridiron.'),
('08-11', 'Saint Clare, virgin', 'Saint Clare of Assisi', '1194, Assisi, Italy', '1253, Assisi', ['television'],
 'Inspired by Saint Francis, Clare left her wealthy family to live in poverty and prayer. She founded the Poor Clares. When soldiers attacked Assisi, she held up the Blessed Sacrament and they fled.\n\nShe is patron of television because, when sick, she saw Mass on the wall of her room.'),
('08-12', 'Saint Jane Frances de Chantal, religious', 'Saint Jane Frances de Chantal', '1572, Dijon, France', '1641, France', [],
 'Jane Frances was a widow and mother of four. Guided by Saint Francis de Sales, she founded the Visitation Sisters, welcoming women who could not bear harsh penances.'),
('08-13', 'Saints Pontian, pope, and Hippolytus, priest, martyrs', 'Saints Pontian and Hippolytus', '', 'c. 235, Sardinia', [],
 'Pope Pontian and the priest Hippolytus had been on opposite sides of a dispute in Rome. Both were exiled to the mines of Sardinia, where they were reconciled before dying as martyrs.'),
('08-14', 'Saint Maximilian Mary Kolbe, priest and martyr', 'Saint Maximilian Kolbe', '1894, Poland', '1941, Auschwitz', ['prisoners', 'journalists', 'families'],
 'Maximilian was a Polish Franciscan who spread devotion to Mary Immaculate through publishing. Arrested by the Nazis and sent to Auschwitz, he offered his life in place of a father condemned to die in the starvation bunker.\n\nHe was killed by lethal injection on 14 August 1941.'),
('08-15', 'Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', [],
 'At the end of her earthly life, Mary was taken up body and soul into heavenly glory. Pope Pius XII defined this dogma in 1950.\n\nMary\'s Assumption is a sign of hope that we too are called to share in the Resurrection of her Son.'),
('08-16', 'Saint Stephen of Hungary', 'Saint Stephen of Hungary', 'c. 975, Hungary', '1038, Hungary', ['Hungary'],
 'Stephen was the first Christian king of Hungary. He organised the Church in his kingdom, built churches and cared for the poor.'),
('08-19', 'Saint John Eudes, priest', 'Saint John Eudes', '1601, France', '1680, Caen, France', [],
 'John Eudes was a French priest and missionary who founded congregations for the formation of priests and the care of women in need. He promoted devotion to the Hearts of Jesus and Mary.'),
('08-20', 'Saint Bernard of Clairvaux, abbot and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Bernard of Clairvaux', '1090, Burgundy, France', '1153, Clairvaux, France', [],
 'Bernard joined the Cistercians with thirty companions and became abbot of Clairvaux. A tireless preacher and adviser of popes, he wrote beautifully on the love of God and the Virgin Mary.\n\nHe is the "Mellifluous Doctor", a Doctor of the Church.'),
('08-21', 'Saint Pius X, pope', 'Saint Pius X', '1835, Riese, Italy', '1914, Rome', [],
 'Giuseppe Sarto, a humble parish priest who became pope, chose the motto "To restore all things in Christ." He encouraged frequent Holy Communion and allowed children to receive it from the age of reason.'),
('08-22', 'Queenship of Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Queenship of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', [],
 'A week after the Assumption, the Church honours Mary as Queen of heaven and earth. Her queenship is one of loving service, sharing in the kingship of her Son.'),
('08-23', 'Saint Rose of Lima, virgin', 'Saint Rose of Lima', '1586, Lima, Peru', '1617, Lima', ['the Americas', 'Peru'],
 'Rose was a Dominican tertiary in Lima who lived a life of prayer and penance in a small hut in her family\'s garden, caring for the sick and poor. She was the first saint of the Americas.'),
('08-24', 'Saint Bartholomew the Apostle', 'Saint Bartholomew the Apostle', '', '1st century', [],
 'Bartholomew is usually identified with Nathanael, whom Jesus called "an Israelite in whom there is no guile." Tradition says he preached in India and Armenia, where he was martyred.'),
('08-25', 'Saint Joseph of Calasanz, priest', 'Saint Joseph Calasanz', '1557, Spain', '1648, Rome', ['schools'],
 'Joseph opened the first free public school in Europe in Rome and founded the Piarists to educate poor children.'),
('08-25', 'Saint Louis', 'Saint Louis of France', '1214, France', '1270, Tunis', ['France'],
 'Louis IX, King of France, was a just ruler, devoted husband and father of eleven, and generous to the poor. He died of disease near Tunis while on crusade.'),
('08-27', 'Saint Monica', 'Saint Monica', 'c. 331, North Africa', '387, Ostia, Italy', ['mothers', 'wives'],
 'Monica was a Christian wife and mother in North Africa who prayed for years for the conversion of her husband and her son Augustine. Her prayers and tears were answered when Augustine was baptised.\n\nShe is a patron of mothers.'),
('08-28', 'Saint Augustine of Hippo, bishop and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Augustine of Hippo', '354, Thagaste, North Africa', '430, Hippo, North Africa', ['theologians', 'printers'],
 'Augustine, born in today\'s Algeria, searched for truth through pleasure and philosophy until he was converted in Milan. He became Bishop of Hippo and one of the greatest teachers of the Church. His *Confessions* begin: "You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our hearts are restless until they rest in you."\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('08-29', 'The Beheading of Saint John the Baptist, martyr', 'The Passion of Saint John the Baptist', '', '', [],
 'John the Baptist rebuked King Herod for taking his brother\'s wife. Herodias held a grudge, and at a banquet her daughter asked for John\'s head on a platter.\n\nJohn died a martyr for truth.'),
('09-03', 'Saint Gregory the Great, pope and doctor', 'Saint Gregory the Great', 'c. 540, Rome', '604, Rome', ['musicians', 'teachers'],
 'Gregory gave up a high government post to become a monk and was elected pope in a time of plague and war. He fed the poor, sent missionaries to England and wrote on the pastoral care of souls. Gregorian chant bears his name.\n\nHe called himself "servant of the servants of God". He is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('09-08', 'Birth of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', [],
 'Nine months after the Immaculate Conception, the Church celebrates the birth of Mary, the dawn of salvation. Her birth announced the coming of Christ, the Light of the world.'),
('09-09', 'Saint Peter Claver, priest', 'Saint Peter Claver', '1580, Spain', '1654, Cartagena, Colombia', ['enslaved people', 'Colombia'],
 'Peter Claver was a Spanish Jesuit who served the enslaved Africans brought to Cartagena in Colombia. For forty years he met the slave ships, bringing food, medicine and the love of Christ, and baptised many.\n\nHe called himself "the slave of the slaves forever".'),
('09-12', 'Holy Name of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Most Holy Name of Mary', '', '', [],
 'The Church honours the holy name of Mary, Mother of God, to whom Christians have always turned in prayer. The feast spread after the victory at Vienna in 1683.'),
('09-13', 'Saint John Chrysostom, bishop and doctor', 'Saint John Chrysostom', 'c. 347, Antioch', '407, Pontus', ['preachers'],
 'John was a priest of Antioch whose eloquence earned him the name Chrysostom, "golden mouth". As Archbishop of Constantinople he challenged the rich and powerful and was exiled. His last words were: "Glory to God for all things."\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('09-15', 'Our Lady of Sorrows', 'Our Lady of Sorrows', '', '', [],
 'The day after the Exaltation of the Holy Cross, the Church remembers Mary standing by the cross of her Son. Simeon had foretold that a sword would pierce her soul.\n\nShe shares in our sufferings and teaches us faithful love.'),
('09-16', 'Saints Cornelius, pope, and Cyprian, bishop, martyrs', 'Saints Cornelius and Cyprian', '', '253 and 258', [],
 'Pope Cornelius and Cyprian, Bishop of Carthage in North Africa, stood together for the unity of the Church and mercy toward those who had lapsed during persecution. Both died as martyrs. They are named in the Roman Canon.'),
('09-17', 'Saint Hildegard of Bingen, Virgin and Doctor', 'Saint Hildegard of Bingen', '1098, Germany', '1179, Bingen, Germany', [],
 'Hildegard was a Benedictine abbess, mystic, composer and writer on medicine and nature. She advised popes and emperors.\n\nPope Benedict XVI named her a Doctor of the Church in 2012.'),
('09-17', 'Saint Robert Bellarmine, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Robert Bellarmine', '1542, Italy', '1621, Rome', ['catechists'],
 'Robert Bellarmine was a Jesuit theologian and cardinal who explained and defended the Catholic faith after the Reformation and wrote catechisms for children.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('09-19', 'Saint Januarius, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Januarius', '', 'c. 305, Pozzuoli, Italy', ['Naples'],
 'Januarius was Bishop of Benevento who was beheaded during the persecution of Diocletian. In Naples a vial of his blood is venerated.'),
('09-20', 'Saint Andrew Kim Taegon, priest, and Paul Chong Hasang and companions, martyrs', 'Saints Andrew Kim Taegon, Paul Chong Hasang and Companions', '', '1839–1867, Korea', ['Korea'],
 'The Church in Korea was begun by lay people who discovered the faith through books. Andrew Kim Taegon was the first Korean priest; Paul Chong Hasang was a lay leader. They and 101 companions were martyred in the 19th century.'),
('09-21', 'Saint Matthew the Evangelist, Apostle, Evangelist', 'Saint Matthew, Apostle and Evangelist', 'Galilee', '1st century', ['accountants', 'tax collectors'],
 'Matthew was a tax collector sitting at his booth when Jesus said, "Follow me." He rose and followed, and later wrote the Gospel that bears his name, showing Jesus as the fulfilment of God\'s promises.'),
('09-23', 'Saint Pio of Pietrelcina (Padre Pio), priest', 'Saint Pio of Pietrelcina (Padre Pio)', '1887, Pietrelcina, Italy', '1968, San Giovanni Rotondo, Italy', ['adolescents', 'stress relief'],
 'Padre Pio was a Capuchin friar who bore the wounds of Christ, the stigmata, for fifty years. Thousands came to him for confession and spiritual guidance. He founded a great hospital, the House for the Relief of Suffering.\n\nHis advice: "Pray, hope and don\'t worry."'),
('09-26', 'Saints Cosmas and Damian, martyrs', 'Saints Cosmas and Damian', 'Arabia', 'c. 287, Syria', ['doctors', 'pharmacists'],
 'Cosmas and Damian were twin brothers and doctors who treated the sick without charge. They were martyred under Diocletian and are named in the Roman Canon.'),
('09-27', 'Saint Vincent de Paul, priest', 'Saint Vincent de Paul', '1581, France', '1660, Paris', ['charities'],
 'Vincent de Paul dedicated his life to the poor. He founded the Congregation of the Mission and, with Saint Louise de Marillac, the Daughters of Charity.\n\nThe Society of Saint Vincent de Paul carries on his work in parishes around the world.'),
('09-28', 'Saint Wenceslaus, martyr', 'Saint Wenceslaus', 'c. 907, Bohemia', '935, Bohemia', ['the Czech Republic'],
 'Wenceslaus was Duke of Bohemia, known for his faith and charity to the poor. He was murdered by his brother on the way to Mass.'),
('09-28', 'Saints Lawrence Ruiz and companions, martyrs', 'Saint Lorenzo Ruiz and Companions', 'Manila, Philippines', '1637, Nagasaki, Japan', ['the Philippines'],
 'Lorenzo Ruiz was a Filipino husband and father who joined Dominican missionaries going to Japan. Arrested, he refused to deny Christ and was martyred in Nagasaki with fifteen companions.'),
('09-29', 'Saints Michael, Gabriel and Raphael, Archangels', 'Saints Michael, Gabriel and Raphael, Archangels', '', '', ['the Church', 'police', 'messengers', 'travellers'],
 'Michael ("Who is like God?") defends God\'s people against evil; Gabriel ("strength of God") announced to Mary that she would bear the Son of God; Raphael ("God heals") guided Tobias and healed his father.\n\nThe archangels remind us of God\'s care through his angels.'),
('09-30', 'Saint Jerome, priest and doctor', 'Saint Jerome', 'c. 347, Dalmatia', '420, Bethlehem', ['Scripture scholars', 'translators', 'librarians'],
 'Jerome was a scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, the Vulgate, used by the Church for centuries. He lived as a monk in Bethlehem. He said: "Ignorance of Scripture is ignorance of Christ."\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
]
